# Portability patches (format and list: docs/UPSTREAM_CHANGES.md).
patch: qtesla-sample-unaligned-load-1
why: UBSan 'load of misaligned address for type uint32_t' (CI run 45610244076): a uint32_t is read through a cast of
why: an unsigned char buffer at a byte offset that is not a multiple of 4 (nbytes is 3 or 4 bytes per sample). Undefined behaviour.
why: The patch copies the 4 bytes one by one into a local uint32_t (a memcpy without needing <string.h>, which these files do not include; the same native-order value the cast load produced)
why: and applies the same mask and the same assignment, so every coefficient and thus every key, signature and KAT is unchanged.
file: upstream/ref/sample.c
count: 1
find: val  = (*(uint32_t *)(buf+pos)) &((1<<PARAM_B_BITS)-1);
replace: { uint32_t qt_; unsigned char *qp_ = (unsigned char *)&qt_; const unsigned char *qs_ = (const unsigned char *)(buf+pos); qp_[0] = qs_[0]; qp_[1] = qs_[1]; qp_[2] = qs_[2]; qp_[3] = qs_[3]; val = qt_ &((1<<PARAM_B_BITS)-1); }
patch: qtesla-aligned-wrapper-keypair
why: Rust passes pk, sk and sm slices at any byte offset, but pack/unpack code in sign.c (and pack.c) reads and writes them through
why: uint32_t*, int16_t*, int32_t* casts: misaligned access, undefined behaviour (UBSan: load/store of misaligned address). The patch renames the
why: upstream function to a static qt_wrap_*_impl and defines the public one in front of it with the same signature: it runs the unchanged
why: function on 8-byte aligned buffers (uint64_t arrays; sm via malloc) and copies the bytes in and out, so the algorithm sees identical bytes
why: and outputs identical bytes. The aligned secret-key copy is zeroized on every exit path; an allocation failure or length overflow returns -1.
file: upstream/ref/sign.c
count: 1
find: int crypto_sign_keypair(unsigned char *pk, unsigned char *sk)
replace: static int qt_wrap_keypair_impl(unsigned char *pk, unsigned char *sk); int crypto_sign_keypair(unsigned char *pk, unsigned char *sk) { uint64_t pkw[(CRYPTO_PUBLICKEYBYTES+7)/8]; uint64_t skw[(CRYPTO_SECRETKEYBYTES+7)/8]; int r; r = qt_wrap_keypair_impl((unsigned char *)pkw, (unsigned char *)skw); memcpy(pk, pkw, CRYPTO_PUBLICKEYBYTES); memcpy(sk, skw, CRYPTO_SECRETKEYBYTES); { volatile unsigned char *qz_ = (volatile unsigned char *)skw; size_t qi_; for (qi_ = 0; qi_ < sizeof skw; qi_++) qz_[qi_] = 0; } return r; } static int qt_wrap_keypair_impl(unsigned char *pk, unsigned char *sk)
patch: qtesla-aligned-wrapper-sign
why: Rust passes pk, sk and sm slices at any byte offset, but pack/unpack code in sign.c (and pack.c) reads and writes them through
why: uint32_t*, int16_t*, int32_t* casts: misaligned access, undefined behaviour (UBSan: load/store of misaligned address). The patch renames the
why: upstream function to a static qt_wrap_*_impl and defines the public one in front of it with the same signature: it runs the unchanged
why: function on 8-byte aligned buffers (uint64_t arrays; sm via malloc) and copies the bytes in and out, so the algorithm sees identical bytes
why: and outputs identical bytes. The aligned secret-key copy is zeroized on every exit path; an allocation failure or length overflow returns -1.
file: upstream/ref/sign.c
count: 1
find: int crypto_sign(unsigned char *sm,
replace: static int qt_wrap_sign_impl(unsigned char *sm, unsigned long long *smlen, const unsigned char *m, unsigned long long mlen, const unsigned char* sk); int crypto_sign(unsigned char *sm, unsigned long long *smlen, const unsigned char *m, unsigned long long mlen, const unsigned char* sk) { uint64_t skw[(CRYPTO_SECRETKEYBYTES+7)/8]; unsigned long long cap = mlen + CRYPTO_BYTES; uint64_t *smw; int r; if (cap < mlen || cap > (unsigned long long)((size_t)-1) - 16) return -1; smw = (uint64_t *)malloc((size_t)((cap + 7) / 8 + 1) * 8); if (smw == NULL) return -1; memcpy(skw, sk, CRYPTO_SECRETKEYBYTES); r = qt_wrap_sign_impl((unsigned char *)smw, smlen, m, mlen, (const unsigned char *)skw); if (r == 0) { if (*smlen > cap) r = -1; else memcpy(sm, smw, (size_t)*smlen); } free(smw); { volatile unsigned char *qz_ = (volatile unsigned char *)skw; size_t qi_; for (qi_ = 0; qi_ < sizeof skw; qi_++) qz_[qi_] = 0; } return r; } static int qt_wrap_sign_impl(unsigned char *sm,
patch: qtesla-aligned-wrapper-open
why: Rust passes pk, sk and sm slices at any byte offset, but pack/unpack code in sign.c (and pack.c) reads and writes them through
why: uint32_t*, int16_t*, int32_t* casts: misaligned access, undefined behaviour (UBSan: load/store of misaligned address). The patch renames the
why: upstream function to a static qt_wrap_*_impl and defines the public one in front of it with the same signature: it runs the unchanged
why: function on 8-byte aligned buffers (uint64_t arrays; sm via malloc) and copies the bytes in and out, so the algorithm sees identical bytes
why: and outputs identical bytes. The aligned secret-key copy is zeroized on every exit path; an allocation failure or length overflow returns -1.
file: upstream/ref/sign.c
count: 1
find: int crypto_sign_open(unsigned char *m,
replace: static int qt_wrap_open_impl(unsigned char *m, unsigned long long *mlen, const unsigned char *sm, unsigned long long smlen, const unsigned char *pk); int crypto_sign_open(unsigned char *m, unsigned long long *mlen, const unsigned char *sm, unsigned long long smlen, const unsigned char *pk) { uint64_t pkw[(CRYPTO_PUBLICKEYBYTES+7)/8]; uint64_t *smw; int r; if (smlen > (unsigned long long)((size_t)-1) - 16) return -1; smw = (uint64_t *)malloc((size_t)((smlen + 7) / 8 + 1) * 8); if (smw == NULL) return -1; if (smlen != 0) memcpy(smw, sm, (size_t)smlen); memcpy(pkw, pk, CRYPTO_PUBLICKEYBYTES); r = qt_wrap_open_impl(m, mlen, (const unsigned char *)smw, smlen, (const unsigned char *)pkw); free(smw); return r; } static int qt_wrap_open_impl(unsigned char *m,
patch: qtesla-gauss-aligned-seed-buffer
why: seed_ex is an unsigned char stack array that is reinterpreted as int64_t* (buf): not guaranteed 8-byte aligned (UB, misaligned load).
why: The patch declares the storage as an int64_t array and keeps seed_ex as a byte pointer to it; sizes and contents are unchanged.
file: upstream/ref/sample.c
count: 1
find: unsigned char seed_ex[PARAM_N*16];
replace: int64_t seed_ex_w_[PARAM_N*2]; unsigned char *seed_ex = (unsigned char *)seed_ex_w_;
